=== layout: two-panels

# *Two Closed Loops*

@left

![Slide image 20](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/cousin_pedigree.drawn.svg)

@right

![Slide image 21](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/cousin_path_both.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Coefficient of Inbreeding*

@left

- An easy way to do this calculation is by determining the number of ancestors in path.
- So for Path 1 above starting at I, that would be
  - --> E --> C --> A --> D --> F
- There are 5 ancestors in this complete path (from I back to I), and we know the probability of a particular allele being transmitted at each step is 0.5, so we can calculate the total probability as
  - F = &#189; &#215; &#189; &#215; &#189; &#215; &#189; &#215; &#189; = (&#189;)5
- ----> or, F = 0.03125 (3.1%).

@right

![Slide image 22](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/cousin_path_a.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Coefficient of Inbreeding*

@left

![Slide image 22](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/cousin_path_both.drawn.svg)

@right

- About 3% of the genome of I will be homozygous with A.
- So, for the next step, we have to consider both parents involved in this inbreeding, namely A and B.
- The paths for B are just the same for A, so once again we have 5 ancester nodes, and the calculated F for B is once again 3.1%.
- Finally, we compute the total probability of homozygosity in this breeding as\:
  - &#8594; F = 3.125% + 3.125% = 6.25%;
- &#8594; therefore the COI of animal/person I is 6.25%.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 23](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/standard_inbreeding_comparison.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Complex pedigree with multiple paths*

@left

![Slide image 24](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/complex_inbreeding_paths.drawn.svg)

@right

- Calculation of inbreeding coefficient in a complex pedigree is more involved because each path contributes to the final inbreeding coefficient.

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Complex pedigree with multiple paths*

@left

![Slide image 24](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/complex_inbreeding_paths.drawn.svg)

@right

- Paths\:
  - EBD\: (&#189;)3
  - ECD\: (&#189;)3
  - ECABD\: (&#189;)5
  - EBACD\: (&#189;)5
- FI = (&#189;)3 + (&#189;)3 + (&#189;)5 + (&#189;)5 = 5/16
  - Individual I is 5/16 less likely to be heterozygous than a non-inbred individual

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 25](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/image_025.png)

- what are the values of p and q?

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 25](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/image_025.png)

- q^2^ = 8.5e-6
- q = sqrt(8.5e-6) = 0.002915 = 2.9e-3
- with inbreeding the expected freq of homoz. recessives is\: q^2^(1 - F) + qF

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 25](assets/lect15e-inbreeding/image_025.png)

- q^2^ = 8.5e-6
- q = sqrt(8.5e-6) = 0.002915
- with inbreeding the expected freq of homoz. recessives is\: q^2^(1 - F) + qF
- 8.5e-6 \* (1 - 1/16) + 0.0029\*1/16
- = 0.00019 = 1.9 x 10-4
- that is 22 times more likely
- F = 1/64 &#8594; 5.3 x 10-5 &#8594; 6 times more likely

